A||N||21||Alex||Argh!!! Damn Wheel Locks!!||||1||
Z||000000||Alex||05-05-2001||08:43 PM||||Hi Guys<BR>I bought a 95 GT a while back and find it's now time to change the wheels and tires.<BR>Problem is that it has wheel locks on the wheels (one per wheel) and I don't have the wrench. <BR>I went to the Pontiac Dealed and they told me  that there were so many different types that they didn't carry any of them but for $35 they'd break them off.<BR>OK, if they can do it so can I and the first one broke apart easily with a few blows from my hand sledge and a big cold chisel. Well, it broke to a point. Now, just the tapered section remains intact and it won't budge.<BR>Not to worry though, I'll get it out of there if I have to cut the wheel.<BR>There are, however, three more ov these damn things and i don't want to spend my life getting them off.<BR>Anybodt ever have to do this and, if so, how did you do it?<BR>Alex<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||05-05-2001||08:52 PM||||The way I remove them when I need to is get some cheap sockets. Find on a size a little small. Just a little. Drive the socket on with a hammer then insert the ratchet handle and just turn them of like a regular nut. I will assume its an 85GT as I think the quit building GT's in 88||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||05-05-2001||08:59 PM||||I used to work at a tire store, and we had a set of lock removal tools.  The one that worked for most of these looked like a regular socket on the outside, and had a tapered cone shape on the inside.  We would hammer the socket on good and tight, then use a breaker bar really slowly and ease off the lugnut.  Make sure you leave the other lugnuts on when doing this, so you only have to break the locknut loose.  Since you probably don't know how to find a socket like that, here's some more ideas...first of all, does the lock stick out past the wheel at all (could you get some pliers on it?  I know normal pliers wouldn't do it, but some stud pliers probably would).  If not, here's a worthy idea:  try using a die grinder to grind a notch on one side of the locknut.  Once you have a notch big enough to fit your chisel in, try and hammer the chisel so you rotate it loose.  I've seen this work on many types of large nuts, never tried on a lugnut.  Third idea...try finding out which socket is just barely too small to go over the locknut.  If the wheel isn't in the way, you could try and hammer this onto the locknut.  You only have to get it on a little bit, and very very gently apply torque with a breaker bar to break it loose.  If it slips, just hammer it on farther until you get it.  Hope something here helps you, I know some of those can be a real bltch.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||05-05-2001||09:01 PM||||Hehe, hey, great idea Tom <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I guess I should have typed a little faster <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||4-WHEEL||05-05-2001||09:39 PM||||I buy a lot of Fiero parts from the Chevy <BR>garage. They sell lock sets for around 15 bucks with a wrench included. They should have the stock number for your Fiero. If that does not work at one time I borrowed a socket<BR>from the Pontiac garage to take a set off. <BR>Of course I had to leave my drivers license <BR>and a $20.00 deposit. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4-Wheel2_4.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||05-05-2001||10:07 PM||||I paid Pontiac to remove the McGuard wheel-locks that came on my Formula after I lost the key. Took them 20 minutes, and $35 sounds like what I paid, but can't remember exact amount. I feel it was well worth it, as I wasn't about to waste at least one day with a hammer and sockets, and chisel, etc. Good luck, hope you get them off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fleabitdog||05-05-2001||10:51 PM||||I just bought a socket like nasco described. I did not want to buy a whole set so I followed a snap on truck to his next stop and bought one off him. It works very well, but cost me 23.00. but that is alright because I have had to use one three times in the last week and a half and know of three more cars i will need it for in the near future. Plus one of the benefits is it does not destroy the locknut. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex||05-06-2001||03:38 AM||||Thanks for all the ideas, guy, but this particular lock nut does not protrude at all. In fact, it's only as long as the stud itself (about 1/4" above the wheel surface and that surface is 3/4" deep in the "grove" of the wheel itself).<BR>What ever wrench is used to get it off, it has a 5 lobed "clover leaf" design which sticks of the face or bussiness end of the tool (like a five sided socket that had the same shape out side as in) and is pushed into rather than over the nut.<BR>It's extreemly hard. I ground a slot in it and tried smacking it with hammer and chisel but just cut up the chisel. <BR>If I could get at the back of the disk, I'd grind off the head of the stud but the dust plate is in the way.<BR>I can't get the wheel assy off because the wheel "captures" the caliper on the inside (prevents you from moving the caliper radialy).<BR>Argh! Damn thing!!<BR>Alex<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||05-06-2001||05:24 AM||||Hehe..there's always the torch method.  Seriously though, is there no room for a socket?  I think you should get the removal tool for this like Fleabit and I described, it's never failed in the past.  If there's no room for it....well....hmm..too late to think of more ideas.  I'll check up on things tomorrow to see if there's any progress or better ideas.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex||05-06-2001||10:23 AM||||Nashco<BR>Nope, no PLACE for a socket. You see, the O.D. of this nut is round, without anything for a socket to grab onto. All the grip features are on the face of the nut.<BR>Alex||||reg||1
Z||000010||Songman||05-06-2001||12:58 PM||||I just took mine down to the local tire retailer and told them to get rid of them. When I came back that afternoon they were gone. No problem...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-06-2001||12:58 PM||||Just tack weld a socket or another lug nut onto the wheel lock lug nuts.  <P>The pound on a socket method only works if the lock lug nuts are not on very tight.  I tried that method once and it did not work.<P>George at the Fieroshop in Richmond, CA welded on another lug nut and that was that.  I actually had the correct tool, but it ripped the outside thread off of the lug nut so it was useless.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000012||TOM||05-06-2001||02:24 PM||||ALEX it doesnt matter that the OD of the lock is round and smooth. The socket teeth will bit into it when you drive it on. Just try it. I know it works.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Nashco||05-06-2001||03:27 PM||||Alex, I think you misunderstood what kind of socket I'm referring to.  The socket that has always worked for me on locknuts is not a regular socket.  The outside of the socket looks like any regular socket, about 1" around and smooth.  The inside of the socket is the different part.  It has a completely smooth cone machined from it, so the clamping diameter gets progressively smaller on the inside.  This allows the socket to work on any diameter round nut.  To have enough clamping force, you must hammer the socket on.  Once you do this, you have to turn it very gently so that you don't lose clamping force or get your socket off-camber.  I know you don't want to buy a 25 dollar tool, but this socket will work.  If there's not enough depth to get it on, you can cut it a little shorter, because as I said there's only one small area of the cone diameter that you actually use.  As long as you have about 1/4" gap around the lugnut and depth to put a socket into, this one will work.<P>Do you happen to have a digital camera?  Maybe we could be more helpful if we knew what you were dealing with.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex||05-07-2001||12:18 AM||||Bryce<BR>Now that does sound like it might do the job.<P>UPDATE: First one is off. The one that I beat to oblivian finaly gave way and came off in pieces. Following advice, i put the other four nuts back on and tightened them. Then, having drilled the beginnings of several holes (carbide bit) which were half into the side of the remaining nut and half into the aluminum, I was able to use a drift punch to turn the remainder of the nut enough that is was free to fall apart and off.<BR>Next one, I'll just be a bit more persistant with hammer and chisel to try turning it off.<BR>Thanks for all your advice guys.<BR>As usual, this is the best buch of friends that a 'friend in need' could ask for.<BR>Alex<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||maryjane||05-07-2001||03:17 AM||||maryjane <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex:<BR><B>Bryce<BR>Now that does sound like it might do the job.<P>UPDATE: First one is off. The one that I beat to oblivian finaly gave way and came off in pieces. Following advice, i put the other four nuts back on and tightened them. Then, having drilled the beginnings of several holes (carbide bit) which were half into the side of the remaining nut and half into the aluminum, I was able to use a drift punch to turn the remainder of the nut enough that is was free to fall apart and off.<BR>Next one, I'll just be a bit more persistant with hammer and chisel to try turning it off.<BR>Thanks for all your advice guys.<BR>As usual, this is the best buch of friends that a 'friend in need' could ask for.<BR>Alex</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Your last sentence said it all, Just hope I'm in a position someday to offer answers instead of just questions,Don & Jane<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000016||Max The Chainsaw||05-07-2001||06:29 AM||||I had this problem as well. My first one came of with a hammer and chisel. The second was a Pain. finally took a carbide drill and drilled the wheel stud out from the inside of the nut, then replaced the stud when I got the wheel off.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||TOM||05-07-2001||05:50 PM||||Alex why do you insist on beating it with a hammer and chisel when you can just use a socket and not sweat it?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Alex||05-07-2001||07:31 PM||||Tom<BR>Well, I guess I "insist" because;<BR>(A) I don't have the conical socket.<BR>(B) The Head of this locknut is so large in diameter that there is only about 1/16" between it and the semicircular 'slot' in the Fiero wheel (not much room to drive a socket inbetween).<BR>(C)The head of the lock nut is only about 1/4" proud above the aluminum surface.<BR>(D) The nut is as hard and brittle as glass. <BR>(E) God bless you all but I'm not convinced you invision the nut the way it is. It's a bear!!<P>I'm really not trying to 'dis you or your advice. It just seem easier to do this than to hunt down and pay for something that I don't think will work IN THIS SITUATION.<BR>Please don't feel slighted, Tom. I really do appreciate your advice and the effort you make in giving it.<BR>There doesn't seem to be an "easy" way to do this and my choice just seems the easiest way for me.<BR>Alex||||reg||1
Z||000019||Nashco||05-07-2001||11:34 PM||||Well, don't forget your time is worth money too.  If you figure you've already spent three hours messing with it, posting, etc...and it's going to be at least another 2 or so, that's 5 hours.  $35 bucks, 5 hours, that's seven bucks an hour.  You're approaching the minimum wage barrier in a hurry Alex!  Look out, Taco Bell is next! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I know where you're coming from, I hate paying if I can do it myself (which has been every single time so far...knock on wood), but there are rare occurences when it is easier to just give in and fork out the cash.  Don't forget when you pay you're paying them to F up their tools, instead of yours.  Tools are the expensive part, labor is cheap.  Good luck man, let us know the progress when/if you get them off.  We're pulling for you!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||Alex||05-08-2001||01:06 AM||||Boy, you nailed it there, Bryce. Hate to pay for something I can do myself. It takes a good bit of frustration to force me into paying for this type of thing.<BR>Having said that, moments after posting my last reply to Tom, I sharpend up my 3/4 chisel and, useing your advice, I hammered a notch and started driving to rotate the nut.<BR>Three minutes later, I had the other three locknuts off!!!<BR>Should have listened to you in the first place. Chalk one up for yourself, Bryce. You da MAN!!!<BR>Once again, thank you friends, one and all.<BR>Now I'm going to see if Taco Bell is hiring :-)<BR>Alex<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||TOM||05-08-2001||11:31 PM||||Well now that thats over. I never said go buy the $35 special socket. I use the cheapest junk sockets I can find. But nevermind.||||reg||1
